{"task_id": "Mbpp/608", "solution": "def bell_Number(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the nth Bell number using the Bell triangle algorithm.\n    The Bell number B(n) represents the number of partitions of a set with n elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # Initialize the first row of the Bell triangle as B(0).\n    # This 'row' variable will hold the previous row of the triangle in each iteration.\n    row = [1]\n\n    # Iterate from 1 up to n to compute subsequent rows of the Bell triangle.\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        # The first element of the new row is the last element of the previous row.\n        new_row = [row[-1]]\n\n        # Compute the remaining elements of the new row.\n        # Each element is the sum of the element to its left in the current row\n        # and the element directly above it in the previous row.\n        for j in range(1, i + 1):\n            new_row.append(new_row[j - 1] + row[j - 1])\n        \n        # Update 'row' to be the newly computed row for the next iteration.\n        row = new_row\n        \n    # The nth Bell number is the first element of the nth row.\n    return row[0]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/610", "solution": "from typing import List, TypeVar\nT = TypeVar('T')\ndef remove_kth_element(input_list: List[T], k: int) -> List[T]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a new list with the k'th element removed.\n    Positive k is 1-indexed; negative k uses Python's 0-indexed style (e.g., -1 for last).\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(input_list)\n\n    # Determine the 0-based index to remove, reconciling 1-indexed positive k\n    # with Python's 0-indexed (including negative) k.\n    if k > 0:\n        idx_to_remove = k - 1\n    else:\n        idx_to_remove = k\n    \n    # Normalize the index to be a non-negative 0-based index for consistent slicing.\n    # This handles Python-style negative indices.\n    if idx_to_remove < 0:\n        idx_to_remove = n + idx_to_remove\n    \n    # Create a new list by concatenating slices before and after the element to be removed.\n    # This ensures the original list is not mutated. Slicing gracefully handles out-of-bounds\n    # indices without raising errors, as per the design constraints.\n    return input_list[:idx_to_remove] + input_list[idx_to_remove+1:]"}
