{"task_id": "Mbpp/622", "solution": "def get_median(a: list, b: list, n: int) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Finds the median of two sorted lists of equal length `n` using an O(log n) algorithm.\"\"\"\n\n    def find_kth_element(arr1, arr2, k_val, start1, end1, start2, end2):\n        \"\"\"\n        Finds the k-th smallest element (1-indexed) in the combined sorted elements\n        of arr1[start1:end1+1] and arr2[start2:end2+1].\n        \"\"\"\n        len1 = end1 - start1 + 1\n        len2 = end2 - start2 + 1\n\n        # Ensure arr1 (current segment) is the shorter array to simplify logic\n        if len1 > len2:\n            return find_kth_element(arr2, arr1, k_val, start2, end2, start1, end1)\n\n        # Base case: if arr1 is exhausted, the k-th element must be in arr2\n        if len1 == 0:\n            return arr2[start2 + k_val - 1]\n        \n        # Base case: if k_val is 1, return the minimum of the first elements\n        if k_val == 1:\n            return min(arr1[start1], arr2[start2])\n\n        # Divide k_val into two parts, considering current segment lengths\n        # pa = number of elements to consider from arr1's current segment\n        # pb = number of elements to consider from arr2's current segment\n        pa = min(k_val // 2, len1)\n        pb = k_val - pa\n\n        # Compare elements at the partition points\n        # If arr1's partition element is smaller or equal, discard arr1's first 'pa' elements\n        if arr1[start1 + pa - 1] <= arr2[start2 + pb - 1]:\n            return find_kth_element(arr1, arr2, k_val - pa, start1 + pa, end1, start2, end2)\n        # Else, discard arr2's first 'pb' elements\n        else:\n            return find_kth_element(arr1, arr2, k_val - pb, start1, end1, start2 + pb, end2)\n\n    # The median of 2n elements is the average of the n-th and (n+1)-th smallest elements (1-indexed).\n    median1 = find_kth_element(a, b, n, 0, n - 1, 0, n - 1)\n    median2 = find_kth_element(a, b, n + 1, 0, n - 1, 0, n - 1)\n\n    return (median1 + median2) / 2.0"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/620", "solution": "def largest_subset(numbers: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the size of the largest subset where, for any pair (a, b) in the subset,\n    a is divisible by b or b is divisible by a. This is implemented using dynamic programming\n    with sorted numbers and specific divisibility rules for zero.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return 0\n\n    def _is_divisible_for_dp_pair(num_larger: int, num_smaller: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Checks if num_smaller divides num_larger, handling zero values safely.\n        This function is tailored for the DP loop where num_smaller is always\n        less than or equal to num_larger from the sorted list.\n        \"\"\"\n        if num_smaller == 0:\n            # If the smaller number is 0:\n            # - If both are 0, they are considered divisible.\n            # - If larger is non-zero and smaller is 0, division by zero is invalid.\n            return num_larger == 0\n        # If num_smaller is non-zero, perform standard modulo operation.\n        return num_larger % num_smaller == 0\n\n    nums = sorted(numbers)\n    n = len(nums)\n\n    # dp[i] stores the length of the largest divisible subset ending with nums[i].\n    dp = [1] * n\n    \n    max_subset_size = 0\n    if n > 0:\n        # A single element itself forms a divisible subset of size 1.\n        max_subset_size = 1 \n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            # Check if nums[j] can be a predecessor for nums[i] in a divisible subset.\n            if _is_divisible_for_dp_pair(nums[i], nums[j]):\n                if dp[j] + 1 > dp[i]:\n                    dp[i] = dp[j] + 1\n        \n        # Update the overall maximum subset size found so far.\n        if dp[i] > max_subset_size:\n            max_subset_size = dp[i]\n            \n    return max_subset_size"}
